<section id="fit"><h2>What fits, and what’s tight</h2><div class="grid">
<div class="card"><h3 class="ok">Fits well</h3><ul>
<li><b>Steinway B</b> (6′11″ × 4′10″): keys toward the kitchen, tail toward the front windows, lid opening west toward the fireplace lounge. 3′5″ to the lounge, 4′1″ to the stair rail, 5′7″ from the tail to the windows.</li>
<li><b>Cigar chairs + tufted bench</b> face each other across the hearth, about 2½ ft off the fireplace.</li>
<li><b>Speakeasy</b> (9′2″ × 13′6″) behind the hinged bookcase: dark walnut paneling, amber sconces and a low brass pendant; a square 4′6″ × 4′6″ bar-height table in the same dark brown stain with 4 matching high-back chairs in the middle (about 2 ft from the table edge to each side wall), and a 36″ × 18″ brass bar cart on the far wall (about 3′8″ from the table). The bookcase swing (about 3 ft) stays clear by about 7″.</li>
<li><b>Secondary office in basement storage:</b> the glass desk + Aeron in a room-within-a-room made from freestanding IKEA-style walls, with every mechanical system left open to reach. <a href="#basement">Plan and photos</a>.</li>
<li><b>Bedroom 2 (kids’ room, 13′9″ × 13′6″):</b> full bed on the north wall, nightstand, kids desk, 6-drawer dresser.</li>
<li><b>Bedroom 3 (teen room, later guest, 17′11″ × 10′5″):</b> sleigh bed, nightstand, dresser, small desk.</li>
<li><b>Primary:</b> king bed on the back wall between nightstands, two tall chests. <b>Dining:</b> table with 6 chairs. <b>Kitchen:</b> 4 counter stools and a buffet by the pantry.</li>
<li><b>TV, console, soundbar, sub</b> on the existing family-room TV wall (outlet and cable are already there).</li>
<li><b>One BendPak HD-9XW-class lift</b> (high-rise, 9,000 lb) in the right bay: Model Y under, 911 Carrera 4S on top at about a 72 in lock. The lift sits behind the 8-ft door panel line (about 10.5 ft in), so a car on top clears the open door. The XT5 parks in the left bay without a lift, which frees the left wall for the backup-power equipment.</li></ul></div>
<div class="card"><h3 class="tight">Tight / check</h3><ul>
<li><b>Sectional size is unmeasured.</b> The corner spot fits a ~138″ × 86″ L with a 65″ chaise; the short leg must stop ~1 ft before the hinged bookcase so the secret door still works.</li>
<li><b>TV distance:</b> ~15 ft from the back seats (~12 ft from the chaise) to a 75″ screen. If that feels far, float the sectional ~3 ft off the back wall with a sofa table, or go 85″.</li>
<li><b>Massage chair</b> needs ~6½ ft when reclined; it sits on the angle by the bath wall.</li>
<li><b>Piano and sun:</b> keep the tail 5+ ft from the front windows (as shown), use UV film or shades, and add a humidity system (Dampp-Chaser).</li>
<li><b>Do not raise the lift to the top lock</b> until the ceiling is taped. Confirm the exact drive-under with BendPak and the post spacing against the stair landing (about 0–0.5 ft of margin).</li>
<li><b>Door tracks:</b> the side doors’ open panels sit at about 8′6″–9′, so the stacked lift stays behind about 10.5 ft from the door. The 12×12 center door is high-lift. Confirm with the installer.</li>
<li><b>High rings and rope:</b> the trusses are not designed for these loads as built. Get a written letter from the truss maker or an engineer before hanging anything. Never drill, notch or hang from a truss chord or web. The rope tops out at about 12′4″–12′6″.</li></ul></div>
<div class="card"><h3 class="no">Still to confirm</h3><ul><li>Existing pieces left with the house are placed at approximate sizes until they are measured.</li><li>Primary bed wall, Bedroom 2 / 3 orientation and the speakeasy layout are checked against the walkthrough videos in the second pass.</li></ul></div></div></section>

<section id="move"><h2>Move-in routes for the piano and sectional</h2><div class="grid">
<div class="card"><h3>Piano</h3><p>Easiest through the <b>6 ft rear French doors</b> in the dining room off the covered deck, then about 15 ft to its spot. Second choice is the 36″ front door with the sidelight; a piano mover takes it in on its side on a board either way. No stairs inside.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Sectional, route A: main stair</h3><p>Straight run, 15 risers, ~4′1″ framed width (~3′5″–3′8″ clear), landing in an 8′3″ hall with room to turn, then open into the family room. <b>Best route</b>, but a <b>stair lift</b> shows on this stair in the listing photos; if it stays, its rail costs about a foot of width. Also check the headroom where the stair passes under the main floor.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Sectional, route B: garage stair</h3><p>From the right side of the garage: a straight 14-riser stair in a 3′11″ hall, then a <b>90° turn through a ~36″ door</b> at the bottom into the storage room, then a 36″ door into the family room. No finished floors to protect, but the bottom turn is the pinch point: pieces deeper than ~34″ or longer than the landing ceiling may not pivot. Measure before moving day.</p></div></div></section>

