<section id="garage"><h2>Garage: CrossFit gym, one lift, mini office at the south window (v3)</h2>
<p>Updated Nov 23, 2026. The 40×50 heated garage has 10 ft walls rising to a flat center of about <b>13 ft</b> over the 12×12 door, with about 11–12 ft at the side-bay centerlines, and a storage loft over the <b>center of the back wall only</b> (about 12–27 ft from the south wall, per the listing photos), with about 8 ft clear under it; the loft and its boxed posts are fixed. The back-left gray workbench and cabinet run stays. All the gray steel shelving is movable and goes to basement storage, and the sellers’ tire rack leaves, which frees the back-right spot. The gym is about <b>670 sf</b>. The 8×8 lifting platform, our own black Xebex-style rack, set up as a 4-post rig with pull-up bars and a 10 ft wall-ball target, and the high rings and climbing rope sit in the 13 ft zone. The cold plunge and a GHD / reverse hyper sit beside it. Cardio (curved treadmill, rower / SkiErg) runs along the left bay. Under the fixed loft only low racks: dumbbells and kettlebells, plates, the bar tree and plyo boxes; the bench and hip-thrust station take the old tire-rack spot. The 4.3 ft walk to the house door and stair stays clear.</p>
<p><b>Stretch / bodyweight / TV area (shared):</b> open floor in the far back-left corner, beside the existing gray workbench and cabinets, with no loft over it (about 10–12 ft). A wall rack holds rollers, bands, mats and light dumbbells, the TV hangs on the south wall, and the back of the office wall is the handstand wall. <b>Mini office:</b> about 10 × 9′8″ (≈99 sf) framed in front of the rear south-facing window, with a new standing desk facing the window for daylight; own insulated lid at about 7′3″ and a glass-lite door on the gym side. <b>Cars:</b> one 4-post lift in the right bay behind the door panels, Model Y under and 911 on top. The XT5 parks in the left bay without a lift, and the Airstream stays in the 12×12. A Tesla Wall Connector is planned on the front wall pier of the right bay. <b>Backup power:</b> three FranklinWH batteries and the aGate on the left wall, with 36 in kept clear; a starter solar array fills the garage south roof, and an outdoor 240V inlet takes a portable dual-fuel generator. Final layout with the installer. <b>Heat and light:</b> the existing gas unit heater and the existing garage lighting stay; nothing added. <b>Cold plunge:</b> plugs into a regular 120V / 15A outlet.</p>
<p><b>High rings and climbing rope (concept, Nov 23):</b> the look is a visible <b>wood or steel mounting bar</b> across the 13 ft ceiling, with the gymnastic rings (straps about 20 in apart, over the 8×8 platform) and a 1.5 in manila climbing rope (with a swivel, over a crash mat) hanging from it. The garage roof is trussed at about 24 in on center, and the attic hatch is at the center of the garage, so the support is built from above: a long engineered beam (a strongback) laid across several trusses in the attic and tied in with structural screws, blocking and bracing, then through-bolted to the visible bar below. Spreading the load this way means no single truss carries a swinging athlete. It is sized for two people at once (one on the rings, one on the rope) with a large dynamic and safety margin. <b>Nothing gets installed without a written letter from the truss manufacturer or an engineer.</b> If they say no, the fallback is a taller freestanding frame bolted to the slab. The rig and the bar stay clear of the fixed loft edge.</p>
<div class="card"><h3>Fit check at true size (Nov 23)</h3><p class="muted">Published vehicle and lift sizes on the 39′6″ × 49′11″ interior (Cubicasa plan), with the XT5, the Model Y under the lift, the 911 on top and the Airstream (planned at 25 ft) all parked. Estimates from the to-scale plan; tape on site.</p><p><b>Which side is the workbench?</b> Standing at the garage doors looking in: the fixed gray workbench and cabinet run is at the <b>rear LEFT</b> (back wall, south half, left of the loft), and the sellers’ tire rack (leaving) is at the rear right under the loft, per listing photos 55–57. The house door is on the right wall and the battery wall is on the left (south) wall.</p><p><b>Verdict:</b> on the true 39′6″ × 49′11″ floor (10 ft walls rising to a 13 ft flat center) everything fits, with 36″ or more along every vehicle side and in front of the batteries. What doesn’t meet 36″: the vehicle fronts sit 9–12″ off the closed doors, the Airstream’s rear is about 34″ from the platform edge, the Model Y has about 8″ each side between the lift posts, and the ring swing comes within about 27″ of the lift posts. Already moved out: all gray steel shelving, the futon and the bikes (to basement storage).</p><table class="costs"><thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>Clearance</th><th>Verdict</th></tr></thead><tbody>
